{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0202"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c017): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c017.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0202"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge geography/cities","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/geography__cities.g2.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",8],["-","small"],["+","towns,"],["=",1],["-","towns"],["+","most"],["=",1],["+","of them small, "],["=",429],["+","\n| Karlsruhe | its fan-shaped street plan | Baden-Württemberg |\n| Braunschweig | the atomic clocks of the PTB | Lower Saxony |\n| Munich | the Oktoberfest | Bavaria |"],["=",508],["+","\n\nKarlsruhe, also in Baden-Württemberg, is famous for its shape. Margrave Charles III William of Baden-Durlach founded the city on 17 June 1715 around the tower of his new palace, and 32 streets radiate outwards from it like the ribs of an open fan – which is why Karlsruhe is known as the „Fächerstadt“, the Fan City. Today it is just as well known as the seat of the Federal Constitutional Court.\n\nBraunschweig, in Lower Saxony, is the city where Germany keeps its time. The Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt (PTB), which grew out of an imperial institute founded in Berlin in 1887, runs four primary caesium atomic clocks there that measure time by the oscillations of atoms with extraordinary precision; the legal time they keep is broadcast by the DCF77 longwave transmitter and sets radio-controlled clocks in millions of homes.\n\nAnd Munich, the Bavarian capital, is famous worldwide for a little more than two weeks a year. The Oktoberfest began on 12 October 1810 with celebrations of the wedding of Crown Prince Ludwig and Princess Therese of Saxe-Hildburghausen, after whom the Theresienwiese is named; today about seven million visitors a year make it the largest folk festival in the world."]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",6],["-","kleiner "],["=",2],["+","die meisten davon klein, "],["=",397],["+","\n| Karlsruhe | ihren fächerförmigen Stadtgrundriss | Baden-Württemberg |\n| Braunschweig | die Atomuhren der PTB | Niedersachsen |\n| München | das Oktoberfest | Bayern |"],["=",484],["+","\n\nKarlsruhe, ebenfalls in Baden-Württemberg, ist für seine Form berühmt. Markgraf Karl III. Wilhelm von Baden-Durlach gründete die Stadt am 17. Juni 1715 rund um den Turm seines neuen Schlosses, und 32 Straßen strahlen von dort aus wie die Rippen eines geöffneten Fächers – deshalb heißt Karlsruhe die „Fächerstadt“. Heute ist es mindestens ebenso bekannt als Sitz des Bundesverfassungsgerichts.\n\nBraunschweig in Niedersachsen ist die Stadt, in der Deutschland seine Zeit macht. Die Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt (PTB), hervorgegangen aus einer 1887 in Berlin gegründeten Reichsanstalt, betreibt dort vier primäre Caesium-Atomuhren, die die Zeit mithilfe von Atomschwingungen höchst genau messen; die gesetzliche Zeit, die sie halten, sendet der Langwellensender DCF77 aus, und nach ihm stellen sich Funkuhren in Millionen Haushalten.\n\nUnd München, die bayerische Landeshauptstadt, ist für gut zwei Wochen im Jahr weltberühmt. Das Oktoberfest begann am 12. Oktober 1810 mit den Feiern zur Hochzeit von Kronprinz Ludwig und Prinzessin Therese von Sachsen-Hildburghausen, nach der die Theresienwiese benannt ist; heute machen es rund sieben Millionen Besucher im Jahr zum größten Volksfest der Welt."]]}]},
